<h2>2. Access to the application and account creation</h2>

<h3>2.1 Eligibility</h3>
<p>Access to MyClash is open to any natural person. Using the application implies that you have the legal capacity to enter into a binding contract.</p>

<h3>2.2 Account creation</h3>
<p>You can create an account in three ways:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Email and password</strong>: you choose a password, and confirm your address by clicking the link we send you.</li>
  <li><strong>Sign-in link</strong> ("magic link"): you provide only an email address, and each sign-in uses a single-use link. No password to remember, and none to lose.</li>
  <li><strong>Sign in with Google (OAuth 2.0)</strong>: see section 3. <strong>This method is not enabled at launch</strong> and will be introduced in a later version.</li>
</ul>
<p>You agree to provide accurate, complete information and to keep it up to date. You are solely responsible for the confidentiality of your credentials and any activity carried out from your account.</p>

<h3>2.3 Creating an organisation</h3>
<p>Creating an account as an event organiser also creates an <strong>organisation</strong>, of which you become the owner. That organisation is the entity that runs events, invites staff, and holds the rosters. Section 4bis sets out what that entails.</p>

<h3>2.4 Taking part without an account</h3>
<p>You may appear on MyClash without ever having created an account, because an event organiser entered you on their roster. You may then use a limited <strong>guest session</strong> — your schedule, your bouts, your workshops — by finding your own name on that event's roster. These Terms apply to that use as well; our <a href="/en/privacypolicy">Privacy Policy</a> explains the arrangement in full.</p>

<h3>2.5 Acceptance of these Terms</h3>
<p>When you create an account, MyClash records <strong>which version</strong> of these Terms and of the Privacy Policy was displayed to you, and when you accepted them. You can view that record at any time in your account settings. Section 12 explains what happens when a new version is published.</p>

<h3>2.6 Multiple accounts and fake profiles</h3>
<p>Creating multiple accounts by the same person in order to circumvent a suspension or manipulate rankings is strictly prohibited.</p>

