<h2>4bis. Obligations of event organisers</h2>
<p>This section applies to you if you create an organisation and run events on MyClash. It does not concern competitors.</p>
<p>To run a competition you enter a roster: names, email addresses and, where applicable, dates of birth, clubs and categories — <strong>for people who have not necessarily signed up to MyClash themselves</strong>. In doing so:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>You are the data controller for that roster</strong>, within the meaning of the GDPR. MyClash acts as your processor, holding and processing that data on your instructions.</li>
  <li><strong>You warrant that you have a lawful basis</strong> for entering those people's data — typically their registration for your event — and that you have informed them of it.</li>
  <li><strong>You undertake to relay requests</strong>: a person on your roster who asks for access, correction or erasure must be able to obtain it from you. We will help; we cannot answer in your place for data you control.</li>
  <li><strong>You enter only what you need.</strong> The free-text notes field attached to a roster row is yours; do not put anything there that you would not be prepared to show the person concerned.</li>
  <li><strong>You do not use these rosters for anything else</strong> — no reuse for prospecting, no transfer to a third party, no purpose other than running your event.</li>
</ul>
<p>Publishing results makes the names, clubs and scores of participants public, as they would be at the venue. Our <a href="/en/privacypolicy">Privacy Policy</a> sets out that rule and its limits; you are responsible for informing your participants of it.</p>

